{"id":4452,"date":"2021-12-06T15:28:33","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:28:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4452"},"modified":"2024-11-25T15:34:37","modified_gmt":"2024-11-25T23:34:37","slug":"1-1-first-flight-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-1-first-flight-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"1.1: First Flight"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae062\" name=\"6a221394ae062\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae1e0\" name=\"6a221394ae1e0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print()<\/code> function and describe its value in debugging (finding and fixing errors) in your code<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add comments to your code and understand the importance of well documented code<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pair with your drone using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.pair()<\/code> function<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Understand how to use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()<\/code>and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.land()<\/code> functions<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this lesson, you will complete the following steps. Make sure that you demonstrate your completed code to your instructor after each step:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hello World! (Using the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print()<\/code> function)<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Using Comments<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Pairing and Printing<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Takeoff! (Your First Flight Test)<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae26e\" name=\"6a221394ae26e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Hello, World<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It&#8217;s time to write your first Python program! This program will use the <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print()<\/code>function. You\u2019re not printing with ink and paper, but you&#8217;ll print to the screen so you can see any output from the program. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Programmers use print statements to track the order in which a computer runs lines of code, which helps them <b>debug<\/b> (find errors in) the code. They also use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print()<\/code>function to check the values of variables (containers that store values) as the program progresses.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Make a new Python file called <em>helloworld.py<\/em> and run this line of code:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(\"Hello, world!\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When you use a print statement, the output will appear in the console. The console is a tool in most IDEs (Integrated Development Environments) that allows you to see specific commands and information about the program as it runs. Most console windows will appear at the bottom of your screen. Here&#8217;s what it looks like in <em>\u201cPython for Robolink\u201d.<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;11756&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The &#8220;Hello World&#8221; program has been used to introduce beginner programmers to a new programming language since the 1970s. Welcome to Python!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae2e8\" name=\"6a221394ae2e8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using Comments<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Comments<\/strong> are ignored by the computer when running your code, so you can write words and sentences as you would in a normal document.\u00a0 You can add comments to your code by beginning the line with a hash symbol (#). These are for single-line only comments, which means that when you start a new line, the program goes back to normal code. <\/span><\/p>\n<p>Comments can also be used by programmers to debug by \u201cturning off\u201d parts of the code by commenting them so they are ignored. This way they can test specific parts of a program to narrow down what the problem may be.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it out with the code below!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\"># print(\"The computer is ignoring this\")\n\nprint(\"The computer is printing this!\")<\/pre>\n<p><em>What do you see?<\/em><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want to write a longer comment, insert the block of code between two sets of triple quotation marks or triple apostrophes. The first set of triple apostrophes is the beginning of the comment, and the second set is the end of the comment. Everything in between will be ignored by the computer. Try it out with the code below:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">'''\nThis is a longer comment that the computer will not read:\nI\nLove\nProgramming\nDrones!\n'''\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae3dc\" name=\"6a221394ae3dc\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Your First Flight Test<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this step, we will learn how to make the drone take off and land. Since the drone will be flying, please make sure you follow all safety precautions and<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> make sure you place your CoDrone EDU away from other objects on the floor.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>The takeoff() function<\/strong>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6139&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Follow the steps below to prepare a new file for this step:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a file called\u00a0<em>flight_test.py (note we use an underscore, you could also use a dash)<\/em><\/li>\n<li>In this new file, add a drone object (you can call it drone or give it a name of your choosing)<\/li>\n<li>Tell the drone object to pair<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>We use an underscore or dash to make the file name readable. It is possible to use a space, but is not good programming practice, as not all interpreters will be able to understand or use file names with spaces. If you are having trouble with these steps, please go back and review the previous step and code.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now we are ready to write our flight test! Write the <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()<\/code><\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">command, which will allow your CoDrone EDU will take off and hover. Note that the takeoff command does not take in any parameters, so the parenthesis should be empty. This is because <\/span><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()<\/code><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> goes through the steps of starting the motors and flying the drone to a preset height. Add the following code to your <em>flight_test.py<\/em>:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\nprint(\"taking off\")<\/pre>\n<p><strong>The land() function<\/strong>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6140&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Much like the<strong> takeoff()<\/strong> function, the <strong>land()<\/strong> function follows a preset routine that will land the drone safely on the ground.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">There are two ways a drone can land. The first is with the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.land()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">command, which will gently bring your CoDrone EDU to the ground.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1726604209477{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"118\" height=\"119\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">The <strong>emergency_stop()<\/strong> should only be used in emergencies or when safety has become a concern. While using \u201cPython for Robolink\u201d, you can press the \u201cStop\u201d button to the right of the menu bar to initiate an emergency stop. In all other circumstances, you should use <strong>land()<\/strong> to bring your drone safely to the ground.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The second is with the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.emergency_stop()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">command. This immediately turns off all the motors, which will crash your CoDrone EDU to the ground. Ideally, this should only be used in emergency situations, and you can program your keyboard\u2019s space bar to act as an emergency stop if it\u2019s hit. For now, we will stick with a simple land command. Below you will find an example of the code with print statements included.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\nprint(\"Paired!\")\ndrone.takeoff()\nprint(\"In the air!\")\nprint(\"Landing\")\ndrone.land()\ndrone.close()\nprint(\"Program complete\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae454\" name=\"6a221394ae454\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Hover<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want your CoDrone EDU to hover for more time before landing, you can use the <strong>hover()<\/strong> function after calling takeoff(<\/span>).<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.hover()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Don&#8217;t forget to type, inside the parentheses, the amount of time in seconds that you would like your CoDrone EDU to hover.<\/em><\/span><\/p>\n<p>Sample code that includes the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.hover()<\/code> function is provided below.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\nprint(\"Paired!\")\ndrone.takeoff()\nprint(\"In the air!\")\nprint(\"Hovering for 5 seconds\")\ndrone.hover(5)\nprint(\"Landing\")\ndrone.land()\ndrone.close()\nprint(\"Program complete\")<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae4c0\" name=\"6a221394ae4c0\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Sleep<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Using the <strong>hover()<\/strong> function is one way to make your program pause for a duration of seconds. You can also use a<strong> sleep()<\/strong> function:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">#The following code will cause the program to wait for 5 seconds.\ntime.sleep(5)<\/pre>\n<p>This function will pause your program. No other commands will be sent until the delay finishes. Enter a number of seconds inside the parentheses to establish the timing of the delay.<\/p>\n<p>The <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep()<\/code> function comes from Python&#8217;s time library. To use the function, add the following import statement to the top of your program:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import time<\/pre>\n<p>While running the program provided in this lesson&#8217;s previous step, replace the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.hover()<\/code> with a\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep()<\/code>. <em>What do you observe?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221394ae509\" name=\"6a221394ae509\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about how to take off, hover, and land your drone just by using code! In the next lesson, we will learn additional flight movements.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text][\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-1-first-flight-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1575,1438,1252,1482,1055,853,144,694,1241,59,171],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2112,2123,2114,2134,2113],"class_list":["post-4452","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-flight-events","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-landing","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-takeoff","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4452","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":54,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4452\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13278,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4452\/revisions\/13278"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4452"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4452"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4452"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}